    },
    "https://jobs.helsinki.fi/job/Helsinki-Doctoral-Researcher-on-Education-Related-Inequalities/1375905057/": {
      "title": "Doctoral Researcher on Education-Related Inequalities",
      "description": "Doctoral Researcher on Education-Related Inequalities Job Details | University of Helsinki We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve the website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login VIEW ALL JOBS VIEW BY CATEGORY Professors, assistant and associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions CAREERS Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login Search by title, unit or a word Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Doctoral Researcher on Education-Related Inequalities The Faculty of Social Sciences of the University of Helsinki invites applications for the position of A DOCTORAL RESEARCHER ON EDUCATION-RELATED INEQUALITIES for a fixed term period of approximately 3.5 years, starting from 18 January 2027 until 30 August 2030 (or as agreed). The position is situated in the Helsinki Institute for Helsinki Institute for Demography and Population Health and is a part of the Research Council of Finland funded project Education – a Universal Solvent? Towards a Cross-Disciplinary Synthesis that investigates educational differences across four research areas 1) intergenerational social stratification, 2) population health, and 3) electoral participation as well as seeks 4) synthesis and synergies between these three research traditions. The appointee will conduct research toward their doctoral thesis in one or more of the four research areas (1–4) defined above, with the aim of producing a doctoral dissertation, typically consisting of three peer-reviewed scientific articles and a summary. Working together with the principal investigator, the appointee will have a key role in the project implementation, including conducting and publishing research jointly, as well as other project tasks, such as maintaining its data infrastructure and communication. Additionally, the work duties include teaching or related activities as agreed with the needs of the Institute (up to 5% of annual working time). The project research takes advantage of two types of data sources. First, Finnish census and population register data that are unique internationally in that they longitudinally combine individual and family data of a full national population for over 50 years. Second type of data used consists of Finnish population representative epidemiological cohort studies and twin studies that contain behavioural, clinical and genetic information, which are also linked to register-based information. The project is led by Hannu Lahtinen. QUALIFICATIONS The appointee to the position must hold a master’s degree in demography, public health/epidemiology, quantitative sociology or political science, philosophy, statistics, population genetics or another relevant field by the beginning of the employment. For a successful applicant, strong quantitative methods skills are required. Following are not considered necessary but can be advantageous in the job: • Experience in working with Finnish or other national register-based data infrastructure. • Experience in working with genotyped data is also considered an advantage, particularly if the applicant specializes in research area 2. • For an applicant specializing in research area 4, also more theoretical or meta-empirical (e.g. meta-analysis and systematic literature review methods) research focus is an option. The appointee must have the ability and motivation to pursue postgraduate studies following a study plan and research proposal. The appointee must either hold a right to pursue a doctoral degree at the University of Helsinki or obtain it within the six-month probationary period. The appointee needs to have a strong command of written English. The appointee is expected to reside in Finland while employed by the University of Helsinki. The International Staff Unit provides assistance in relocation. WHAT WE OFFER We are an equal opportunity employer and offer an attractive and diverse workplace in an inspiring environment with a variety of development opportunities and benefits. The appointee will receive active research feedback, thesis supervision and similar support within the research project and wider research group, as well as opportunities for international collaboration and scientific publishing. The starting monthly gross salary range will be approximately €2,700-€3,300 depending on the appointee’s qualifications and experience. In addition, University of Helsinki offers comprehensive services to its employees, including occupational health care, standard Finnish pension benefits, and opportunities for professional development. Further information can be obtained online . The employment contract will include a probationary period of six months. Finland is a member of the European Union, has high-quality free schooling (also in English) and generous family benefits and healthcare. Finland and the Helsinki region feature leading research and innovation facilities, clean environment, affordable accommodation, and excellent international, national, and local transport connections. For more information about working at the University of Helsinki and living in Finland . HOW TO APPLY Please submit your application, together with the required attachments, through the University of Helsinki Recruitment System via the link “Apply now”. Use your current email address to register to the Recruitment System. Applicants who are currently employed by the University of Helsinki are requested to submit their application via the link “Employee login”. The closing date for applications is 19.11. 2026 . The following English-language documents must be enclosed with applications : 1) Curriculum vitae 2) Numbered list of publications (if any) 3) Statement of interest (1-2 pages) outlining applicant's a) skills and research experience, and b) specific interests and planned contribution with regard to the project aims. Detailed information on how to structure the Curriculum vitae and list of publications can be obtained online. FURTHER INFORMATION Further information about the position and the research project may be obtained from Academy Research Fellow Hannu Lahtinen, hannu.lahtinen@helsinki.fi. In case you need support with the recruitment system, please contact recruitment@helsinki.fi. The Faculty of Social Sciences is Finland’s leading research and education institute in the social sciences, and the country’s most diverse social sciences faculty in terms of the range of its disciplines and fields of research. The Faculty has a strong international research profile and an international master’s programme, and several of its disciplinary units have been rated among the top 50 in the world. The faculty has approximately 550 research and teaching staff, and each year it awards some 350 bachelor’s degrees, 400 master’s degrees and more than 50 doctoral degrees. Do you have a helsinki.fi username and a valid employment contract, a grant researcher's contract, a visiting researcher's or visiting professor's contract at the University of Helsinki? Log in here to apply to our open positions. Employee Login Open position Due Date: 19.11.2026 (23.59 Europe/Helsinki time) Unit: Faculty of Social Sciences Sub-unit: Research units ID: 5292 Working for the world Science has the power to change the world. Join one of the best research universities in the world – an international community united through bold thinking, science, research, and learning, with over 30,000 students and 8,000 employees. We are defined by our multidisciplinary approach. This, combined with a dedication for cross-border collaboration, gives us our potential to find real solutions and build hope for a better, more sustainable future. We are united by our values – truth, bildung, freedom, and inclusivity – as well as our passion for work that truly matters. Join us: work for the future – work for the world. Any questions? See our recruitment system FAQ Apply now » Find similar jobs: Teaching and research positions Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. CONTACT Data protection Contact us WHY US? Why University of Helsinki Why Finland CAREER SITES Academic careers Expert careers FACULTIES AND UNITS Flamma intranet Faculties and Units © University of Helsinki 2022 × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Required Cookies These cookies are required to use this website and can't be turned off. Required Cookies Show More Details Required Cookies Provider Description Enabled SAP as service provider We use the following session cookies, which are all required to enable the website to function: \"route\" is used for session stickiness \"careerSiteCompanyId\" is used to send the request to the correct data centre \"JSESSIONID\" is placed on the visitor's device during the session so the server can identify the visitor \"Load balancer cookie\" (actual cookie name may vary) prevents a visitor from bouncing from one instance to another Cookies from provider SAPasserviceprovider are required and cannot be turned off Advertising Cookies These cookies serve ads that are relevant to your interests. You may freely choose to accept or decline these cookies at any time. Note that certain functionality that these third parties make available may be impacted if you do not accept these cookies. Consent to all Advertising Cookies Show More Details Advertising Cookies Provider Description Enabled Google Tag Manager Google Tag Manager is a tag management system for conversion tracking, site analytics, remarketing and more. Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider GoogleTagManager Google Analytics Google Analytics is a web analytics service offered by Google that tracks and reports website traffic. Cookie Information Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider GoogleAnalytics LinkedIn LinkedIn is an employment-oriented social networking service. We use the Apply with LinkedIn feature to allow you to apply for jobs using your LinkedIn profile. Opting out of LinkedIn cookies will disable your ability to use Apply with LinkedIn. Cookie Policy Cookie Table Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider LinkedIn Confirm My Choices Accept All Cookies"
    },
    "https://jobs.helsinki.fi/job/Helsinki-Post-doctoral-researcher-in-computational-genomics-for-predictive-medicine/1374866357/": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral researcher in computational genomics for predictive medicine",
      "description": "Post-doctoral researcher in computational genomics for predictive medicine Job Details | University of Helsinki We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve the website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login VIEW ALL JOBS VIEW BY CATEGORY Professors, assistant and associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions CAREERS Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login Search by title, unit or a word Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Post-doctoral researcher in computational genomics for predictive medicine Post-doctoral researcher in computational genomics for predictive medicine The Barrett lab at the University of Helsinki is recruiting a post-doctoral researcher in computational genomics for predictive medicine. We are jointly affiliated with the ELLIS Institute Finland , the Institute for Molecular Medicine Finland (FIMM) , and the Faculty of Medicine at the University of Helsinki. We analyze multiomic data to discover, understand, and measure mechanistically meaningful subtypes of complex human diseases. We believe that characterizing diseases not by their observed clinical phenotype, but by their molecular and biological endotype, will enable better treatment for patients. This long-held ambition in biomedicine is now poised to be realized due to: •    National research biobanks that collectively include millions of individuals with matched biosamples and detailed electronic health records. •    Large and growing multiomic data sets in samples from these biobanks, including genomics, proteomics, metabolomics, and transcriptomics. •    New machine learning and AI methods to build multiomic models that stratify disease risk, progression, and treatment response by molecular endotypes. Find examples of research areas and possible projects on our website . Responsibilities •    Carry out research in computational genomics for predictive medicine: propose hypotheses, write code, think about and discuss results, and interpret findings. •    Collaborate closely with other members of the team, other researchers at the ELLIS Institute and University of Helsinki, and international partners. •    Share scientific results via publications and presentations at local and international conferences, and contribute more broadly to open science (e.g. via pre-prints and open source software). •    Design projects that answer important questions in predictive medicine. •    Contribute to mentoring and training the next generation of scientists. Qualifications, experience, and skills These items are most important for this role. We expect candidates to fulfill most of these: •    Motivation to work collaboratively on hard scientific problems with a pragmatic focus on getting things done. •    A PhD in computational biology, data science, bioinformatics, or a related discipline. •    A strong publication record. •    Proficiency in programming for data analysis in either Python or R. •    Research experience in computational biology and/or data science. •    Excellent written and spoken English. Any of the following are nice experience to have, but not essential. Please don’t be put off if you don’t have these: •    Experience mentoring more junior colleagues. •    Working in high-performance or cloud compute environments. •    Working on machine learning problems (e.g. with frameworks such as PyTorch or TensorFlow). •    Research with health care and/or genomics data. •    Developing software in a small team environment with code review and version control. •    Using agentic AI tools (e.g. from Anthropic and OpenAI) while still retaining your own thinking process (see here for more about how we use AI). What we offer This is an opportunity to be one of the first members of a newly established laboratory led by Professor Jeffrey Barrett, who has over 20 years’ experience leading genomics research teams. You will benefit from hands-on supervision from Professor Barrett as well as active participation in the intellectual environments at both FIMM (genomics and molecular medicine) and the ELLIS Institute (fundamentals of applied AI). Our team has access to biobank data sets which cumulatively include 2 million individuals (FinnGen, UK Biobank, All of Us, Estonian Biobank). We have access to both local supercomputer facilities as well as extensive cloud computing resources. We also routinely generate new ’omics data in samples available from the Finnish biobank network. The position is for a fixed term, with a maximum duration of three years. A trial period of six months will be applied. The salary is based on the salary system of the Finnish universities . The starting salary is typically around 3800-4000 EUR per month, depending on the appointees’ qualifications and experience. The University of Helsinki offers comprehensive services to its employees, including occupational health care, support in access to high quality sports facilities, and many opportunities for professional development. Standard Finnish pension benefits are provided for university employees. How to apply Please submit your application through the University of Helsinki recruitment system by November 19 , 2026. Your application should include: •    A one-page cover letter describing why you’re interested in this role. Please don’t use AI for this! It’s important to explain in your own voice. •    CV, including publications and github link if applicable •    Contact information for two references For more information regarding the position or the research goals of the group, please contact Jeff at jeffrey.c.barrett(at)helsinki.fi. More information A diverse and equitable study and work culture is important to us. That is why we do our best to promote an inclusive university community. We encourage all qualified applicants from diverse backgrounds to apply for our positions. Click this link to read about accessibility and inclusivity at our University. JOIN US TO BUILD A BETTER WORLD – TOGETHER! #HelsinkiUniCareers The Institute for Molecular Medicine Finland (FIMM) is an international research unit focusing on human genomics and personalized medicine at the Helsinki Institute of Life Science (HiLIFE) of the University of Helsinki - a leading Nordic university with a strong commitment to life science research. As part of Academic Medical Center Helsinki in Meilahti campus FIMM collaborates locally with the Faculty of Medicine, Helsinki University Hospital and National Institute for Health and Welfare. FIMM is part of the Nordic EMBL Partnership for Molecular Medicine, composed of the European Molecular Biology Laboratory (EMBL) and the centres for molecular medicine in Norway, Sweden and Denmark, and the EU-LIFE Community. ELLIS (European Laboratory for Learning and Intelligent Systems) is a major European initiative in AI with a mission to strengthen Europe’s AI landscape by connecting top researchers, attracting talent, and fostering strategic collaborations. ELLIS unites leading machine learning researchers in Europe and operates as a multi-centric, pan-European AI Lab, currently spanning 47 research sites. ELLIS Institute Finland was launched in 2025 and is the second institute in the ELLIS network , partnering with Finnish universities. We develop machine learning through fundamental research, with a particular focus on the fundamentals of applied AI: making machine learning more widely and effectively applicable, and better at working with people. AI for Research is one part of this focus. Our faculty works across a broad range of areas, including data-efficient probabilistic machine learning, generative modelling, amortized inference, interactive and cooperative machine learning, and transformative multidisciplinary collaboration. The institute will have a small number of strategic focus areas and the first one is AI for Health, spearheaded by a project to build a nationwide foundation model to support better decision-making in healthcare. Do you have a helsinki.fi username and a valid employment contract, a grant researcher's contract, a visiting researcher's or visiting professor's contract at the University of Helsinki? Log in here to apply to our open positions. Employee Login Open position Due Date: 19.11.2026 (23.59 Europe/Helsinki time) Unit: Helsinki Institute of Life Science Sub-unit: Institute for Molecular Medicine Finland (FIMM) ID: 5269 Working for the world Science has the power to change the world. Join one of the best research universities in the world – an international community united through bold thinking, science, research, and learning, with over 30,000 students and 8,000 employees. We are defined by our multidisciplinary approach. This, combined with a dedication for cross-border collaboration, gives us our potential to find real solutions and build hope for a better, more sustainable future. We are united by our values – truth, bildung, freedom, and inclusivity – as well as our passion for work that truly matters. Join us: work for the future – work for the world. Any questions? See our recruitment system FAQ Apply now » Find similar jobs: Teaching and research positions Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. CONTACT Data protection Contact us WHY US? Why University of Helsinki Why Finland CAREER SITES Academic careers Expert careers FACULTIES AND UNITS Flamma intranet Faculties and Units © University of Helsinki 2022 × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Required Cookies These cookies are required to use this website and can't be turned off. Required Cookies Show More Details Required Cookies Provider Description Enabled SAP as service provider We use the following session cookies, which are all required to enable the website to function: \"route\" is used for session stickiness \"careerSiteCompanyId\" is used to send the request to the correct data centre \"JSESSIONID\" is placed on the visitor's device during the session so the server can identify the visitor \"Load balancer cookie\" (actual cookie name may vary) prevents a visitor from bouncing from one instance to another Cookies from provider SAPasserviceprovider are required and cannot be turned off Advertising Cookies These cookies serve ads that are relevant to your interests. You may freely choose to accept or decline these cookies at any time. Note that certain functionality that these third parties make available may be impacted if you do not accept these cookies. Consent to all Advertising Cookies Show More Details Advertising Cookies Provider Description Enabled Google Tag