    "https://jobs.helsinki.fi/job/Helsinki-Doctoral-Researcher-in-Archaeobotany/1375698857/": {
      "title": "Doctoral Researcher in Archaeobotany",
      "description": "Doctoral Researcher in Archaeobotany Job Details | University of Helsinki We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve the website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login VIEW ALL JOBS VIEW BY CATEGORY Professors, assistant and associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions CAREERS Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login Search by title, unit or a word Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Doctoral Researcher in Archaeobotany The University of Helsinki invites applications for a Doctoral Researcher in Archaeobotany for a fixed-term position of 36 months in the Research Council of Finland-funded project NeoPlant: “ Neolithic Plant Use, Cultivation and Cultural Roles of Plants in Finland, ca. 3000-1500 BCE”. The position is based at the Faculty of Arts & Cultures at the Department of Archeology, University of Helsinki in Finland, and the successful candidate will join the research group led by Dr. Mia Lempiäinen-Avci. About the project NeoPlant investigates one of the major transformations in human history: the transition from hunting and gathering to food production and farming. The project focuses on Neolithic Finland, where hunting, fishing and gathering continued to play an important role while new practices related to cultivation, animal husbandry and food production gradually emerged. The project examines how plants were used during this transition and what archaeobotanical evidence can reveal about changing subsistence strategies, environments and everyday life. Particular attention is paid to exceptionally well-preserved archaeological contexts, including wetland sites, which provide new possibilities for studying plant use in prehistoric northern Europe. NeoPlant combines archaeobotanical analysis of plant macroremains with archaeological and natural-scientific approaches, including radiocarbon dating, stable isotope analyses and ancient DNA. The project aims to develop a more detailed understanding of when, how and under what circumstances cultivation became part of human subsistence strategies in Finland. About the position The Doctoral Researcher will conduct an independent PhD project within the broader research framework of NeoPlant. The precise research questions and focus of the doctoral dissertation will be developed together with the Principal Investigator, taking into account the successful candidate’s expertise and research interests. The doctoral project is expected to have archaeobotanical plant macroremain analysis as its primary methodological approach. The researcher will work particularly with archaeological plant remains and datasets from Neolithic contexts in Finland and will participate in the collection, processing, analysis and interpretation of archaeobotanical material. The main tasks include: conducting doctoral research within the themes of the NeoPlant project; analysing and identifying archaeological plant macroremains; participating in archaeological sampling and sample processing; managing and analysing archaeobotanical and archaeological datasets; integrating archaeobotanical results with other archaeological and natural-scientific evidence; publishing research results in international peer-reviewed journals; presenting research at national and international conferences; participating in the activities, meetings and collaborative work of the NeoPlant research group; and completing a doctoral degree at the University of Helsinki. Training in specialised archaeobotanical methods will be provided where necessary. Qualifications We are looking for a motivated researcher interested in archaeobotany, prehistoric archaeology and human–plant relationships. The successful candidate is expected to have: a Master’s degree or equivalent in archaeology, archaeobotany, palaeoecology, biology, environmental archaeology or another relevant discipline; a strong interest in prehistoric plant use and the transition from hunting and gathering to farming; the ability and motivation to undertake doctoral research and complete a PhD within the project period; good written and spoken English; the ability to work both independently and as part of an interdisciplinary research team. Previous experience in archaeobotanical analysis, particularly the identification of plant macroremains, is considered an advantage but is not an absolute requirement. Experience in archaeological fieldwork, laboratory work, microscopy, quantitative methods, GIS, palaeoecological methods or other relevant natural-scientific approaches is also considered an asset. Knowledge of Finnish or Swedish is not required. Research environment The successful candidate will become part of an interdisciplinary research environment combining archaeology, archaeobotany and natural-scientific approaches to the human past. The NeoPlant project collaborates with researchers in Finland and internationally and provides opportunities for methodological training, research visits, conference participation and international networking. The researcher will have access to archaeobotanical laboratory facilities and comparative botanical collections required for the identification of plant macroremains. The project values a collaborative research culture in which team members contribute to the development of research questions, methods and interpretations across disciplinary boundaries. E mployment The position is full-time and fixed-term for 36 months. The employment will follow the terms and salary system of the University of Helsinki, being between 3400-3900 euros per month. The position includes a 6  month trial period in accordance with the University of Helsinki regulations. The preferred starting date is 1.1.2027 , or as agreed. How to apply Please submit your application through the University of Helsinki recruitment system. The application should include: a motivation letter describing your research interests, relevant experience and reasons for applying for the position (maximum 2 pages); a curriculum vitae; a list of publications, if applicable; a copy of your Master’s degree certificate and transcript of studies; and contact information for two academic referees. In the motivation letter, applicants are encouraged to briefly describe what aspects of Neolithic plant use, subsistence or the transition to farming they would be particularly interested in investigating within the NeoPlant project. A complete doctoral research proposal is not required at the application stage. The deadline for applications is 30.11.2026 (23:59PM). Please note: Another doctoral researcher position in archaeobotany is expected to open in spring 2027 as part of the ERC-funded PlantStories project, led by Dr Mia Lempiäinen-Avci. PlantStories investigates the roles of plants in everyday life and society in medieval northern Europe, with a particular focus on rye and buckwheat. Candidates interested in archaeobotany and human–plant relationships are encouraged to keep an eye out for this upcoming opportunity. For further information about the position(s) and the NeoPlant project, please contact: Dr. Mia Lempiäinen-Avci Principal Investigator, NeoPlant Faculty of Humanities, Department of Cultures Archaeology University of Helsinki Finland mia.m.lempiainen-avci@helsinki.fi Do you have a helsinki.fi username and a valid employment contract, a grant researcher's contract, a visiting researcher's or visiting professor's contract at the University of Helsinki? Log in here to apply to our open positions. Employee Login Open position Due Date: 30.11.2026 (23.59 Europe/Helsinki time) Unit: Faculty of Humanities Sub-unit: Department of Cultures ID: 5293 Working for the world Science has the power to change the world. Join one of the best research universities in the world – an international community united through bold thinking, science, research, and learning, with over 30,000 students and 8,000 employees. We are defined by our multidisciplinary approach. This, combined with a dedication for cross-border collaboration, gives us our potential to find real solutions and build hope for a better, more sustainable future. We are united by our values – truth, bildung, freedom, and inclusivity – as well as our passion for work that truly matters. Join us: work for the future – work for the world. Any questions? See our recruitment system FAQ Apply now » Find similar jobs: Teaching and research positions Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. CONTACT Data protection Contact us WHY US? Why University of Helsinki Why Finland CAREER SITES Academic careers Expert careers FACULTIES AND UNITS Flamma intranet Faculties and Units © University of Helsinki 2022 × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Required Cookies These cookies are required to use this website and can't be turned off. Required Cookies Show More Details Required Cookies Provider Description Enabled SAP as service provider We use the following session cookies, which are all required to enable the website to function: \"route\" is used for session stickiness \"careerSiteCompanyId\" is used to send the request to the correct data centre \"JSESSIONID\" is placed on the visitor's device during the session so the server can identify the visitor \"Load balancer cookie\" (actual cookie name may vary) prevents a visitor from bouncing from one instance to another Cookies from provider SAPasserviceprovider are required and cannot be turned off Advertising Cookies These cookies serve ads that are relevant to your interests. You may freely choose to accept or decline these cookies at any time. Note that certain functionality that these third parties make available may be impacted if you do not accept these cookies. Consent to all Advertising Cookies Show More Details Advertising Cookies Provider Description Enabled Google Tag Manager Google Tag Manager is a tag management system for conversion tracking, site analytics, remarketing and more. Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider GoogleTagManager Google Analytics Google Analytics is a web analytics service offered by Google that tracks and reports website traffic. Cookie Information Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider GoogleAnalytics LinkedIn LinkedIn is an employment-oriented social networking service. We use the Apply with LinkedIn feature to allow you to apply for jobs using your LinkedIn profile. Opting out of LinkedIn cookies will disable your ability to use Apply with LinkedIn. Cookie Policy Cookie Table Privacy Policy Terms and Conditions Consent to cookies from provider LinkedIn Confirm My Choices Accept All Cookies"
